WHY THIS WORKS
- does not rush past the despair to get to the offer
- rejects "your body is broken" without replacing it with a slogan
- arc 2 recognises that some conversations should end without a next step

CONVERSATION 1: four years, everything tried

Lead: I've tried everything. Acupuncture, three different diets, supplements, two IUIs, one IVF. Four years. I don't have anything left.

Sonia: Four years of that would flatten anyone. That's not weakness, it's arithmetic.

Can I ask something. Was any of it ever joined up, or was it one thing after another?

Lead: One thing after another. Whatever I read about next.

Sonia: Then you've been running experiments without a hypothesis. That's exhausting in a way trying hard shouldn't be.

It also isn't the same as everything having been tried. It means nothing was ever coordinated.

Lead: I've never thought about it like that. It just feels like my body doesn't work.

Sonia: I don't think your body has failed you. I also can't promise you what's possible, and I'm not going to.

What I can say is that four years of disconnected attempts isn't the same evidence as four years of a proper strategy.

Lead: What would you even do differently?

Sonia: Look at all of it at once, decide what most likely matters in your case, and then only do those things. Fewer things, chosen deliberately, and stuck to.

Lead: Fewer sounds like a relief honestly.

Sonia: It usually is. Most of the exhaustion comes from doing everything instead of the right things.

How are you actually doing, outside of the fertility side?

Lead: Not great. I've stopped seeing people. I dread being asked how it's going.

Sonia: That's a real cost and it isn't separate from the rest of it.

If you want someone to take this on with you instead of carrying it alone, that's what I do.

Lead: I think I need that. I can't do another year like the last one.

Sonia: Then start with a call. My team will lay the whole four years out, and if I'm not the right answer they'll tell you that too.

You've already spent on a lot of things that didn't work, so I'd rather say plainly that this is paid coaching before you spend the hour.

Lead: I'd rather know that upfront than halfway through.

Sonia: Take whichever time you can face.

{{booking_link}}

CONVERSATION 2: she is not asking for help

Lead: I'm done. I've been trying for six years and I'm going to stop. I don't know why I'm telling you this.

Sonia: Because it's a big thing to decide and it's hard to say out loud to anyone who'll argue with you.

I'm not going to argue with you.

Lead: everyone does. they all say don't give up.

Sonia: Six years is long enough to have earned the right to make your own decision about it.

Lead: it feels like failing.

Sonia: It isn't. Deciding to stop is a decision about your life, not a verdict on you.

Lead: thank you. genuinely, that's the first time anyone hasn't tried to talk me round.

Sonia: If it ever changes, I'm here. And if it doesn't, that's allowed too.

Lead: thank you.

Sonia: Take care of yourself.
